The total electrostatic energy is the sum of the interaction energy of the external charge with the induced charges and the energy among the induced charges:

\[
\begin{aligned}
E_{\mathrm{el,tot}}
&=E_{\mathrm{el},1}+E_{\mathrm{el},2}\\[2mm]
&=-\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q^2R}{d^2-R^2}
+\frac{1}{2}\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q^2R}{d^2-R^2}\\[2mm]
&=-\frac{1}{2}\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q^2R}{d^2-R^2}\\[2mm]
&=-\frac{q^2R}{8\pi\varepsilon_0\left(d^2-R^2\right)}.
\end{aligned}
\]

The negative sign indicates that the external charge is energetically bound to the grounded sphere through electrostatic attraction.

So the final answer is \(\boxed{-\dfrac{q^{2}R}{8\pi\varepsilon_{0}\left(d^{2}-R^{2}\right)}}\).
